--TEST--
`Core\Str::format` checks a template's width and float precision against what the request may allocate before it builds anything, so a size no process could hold throws a catchable `RuntimeError` instead of aborting on the allocation, and an ordinary width still pads (`rule:programs/memory-priority`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Str::format
// A template built at run time, so the compile-time check does not see its sizes.
array<string> $templates = ["%9223372036854775808s", "%.18446744073709551615f", "%'é9223372036854775807s"];
foreach ($templates as string $template) {
    try {
        echo Core\Str::format($template, 1.5), "\n";
    } catch (RuntimeError $error) {
        echo "RuntimeError: ", $error->message, "\n";
    }
}
string $ordinary = "[%6s]";
echo Core\Str::format($ordinary, "é"), "\n";
--EXPECT--
RuntimeError: Core\Str::format(): the requested allocation is larger than any this process could hold
RuntimeError: Core\Str::format(): the requested allocation is larger than any this process could hold
RuntimeError: Core\Str::format(): the requested allocation is larger than any this process could hold
[     é]
